<Layout 
  title="About - Matt Hurley" 
  description="Educator-first approach to software development, focusing on practical systems, ethical design, and real-world constraints."
>
  <div class="container">
    <h1>About</h1>

    <section class="section">
      <p>
        I am an educator by profession, currently serving in senior leadership, where my work centres on student wellbeing, engagement, and the systems that support both students and staff to thrive.
      </p>
      <p>
        Over time, it became clear that many of the challenges schools face are not issues of intent or effort, but of structure. Fragmented tools, manual processes, duplicated data, and systems not designed for real school contexts often create unnecessary friction — particularly in areas as critical as pastoral care, behaviour support, and student engagement.
      </p>
      <p>
        My first major software project, Sophia STARS, emerged directly from this reality.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sophia STARS was developed as an internal platform to support consistent, ethical, and privacy-first tracking of student engagement and wellbeing. It was designed around real workflows used by teachers and leadership teams, with a strong emphasis on role-based access, clarity of information, and responsible data use. The goal was not surveillance or compliance, but better visibility, earlier support, and more informed pastoral responses.
      </p>
      <p>
        Building Sophia STARS fundamentally changed how I thought about software.
      </p>
      <p>
        What began as a practical solution to a local problem evolved into a broader approach to system design: start with people, respect constraints, prioritise clarity, and build tools that quietly do their job well. That mindset has since carried into other independent projects, from education platforms and learning tools to system utilities and public-facing web applications.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Core Principles</h2>
      <p>
        Across all projects, the same principles apply:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>software should reduce cognitive load, not add to it</li>
        <li>systems must respect privacy and governance by default</li>
        <li>tools should be reliable, understandable, and built to last</li>
        <li>technology should serve real needs in real contexts</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>This Site</h2>
      <p>
        This site brings together those projects and reflections — not as a commercial portfolio, but as a record of learning, iteration, and building software informed by leadership and teaching experience.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you find the work interesting or have questions about the approaches documented here, feel free to reach out via the <a href="/contact">contact page</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
